[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where does the persistent user identifier come from? → Server-issued (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Server-issued it is. Next: how long the identifier survives and where it lives — this determines both the module's
  interface and its security posture.

  The identifier will be readable by any script on the page regardless of choice, so none of these are a place for
  secrets; the fork is purely about lifetime.

  - [1mIn-memory only[0m — a module variable. Cleared on every reload or tab close, so the user re-authenticates constantly.
    Smallest attack surface, worst ergonomics. Notably, this is the only option that does [3mnot[0m need the identifier
    written anywhere a later visitor can read it.
  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — survives reloads and in-tab navigation, dies when the tab closes, and is not shared between tabs.
    Matches most people's intuition for "logged in for this visit."
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives browser restarts indefinitely and is shared across tabs. Best ergonomics, but the ID sits on
    disk until something explicitly clears it, which means a shared or borrowed machine keeps showing the last user's
    ID to whoever opens the browser next. Needs an explicit logout path that clears it.

  This one is genuinely cheap to change later if the storage is behind a small module interface — which is what I'd
  design regardless, so callers never touch [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m directly. My recommendation is [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m: it gives you
  persistence across reloads and it expires on its own, so a stale identity can't outlive the visit.
